{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"4170c93e-b82a-4dad-a326-f2ed38e1a03e","title":"Superposition of two waves: constructive and destructive"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"4170c93e-b82a-4dad-a326-f2ed38e1a03e","step":{"id":"s1","script":"When waves overlap, add their displacements at each position. Compare waves with matching crests on the left and opposite displacements on the right.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"matching","region":"left","frame":{"x":[0,6.2832],"y":[-2.5,2.5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"position","y_label":"displacement"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"wave_a","y":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.2832],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[3.14,2.2],"text":"Matching crests"}]},{"op":"figure","id":"opposing","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,6.2832],"y":[-2.5,2.5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"position","y_label":"displacement"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"wave_b","y":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.2832],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[3.14,2.2],"text":"Opposite displacements"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"4170c93e-b82a-4dad-a326-f2ed38e1a03e","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Identical, in-phase waves reinforce. At every position, equal displacements add, so the resultant has twice the amplitude.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"reinforce","on":"matching","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"double_wave","y":"2*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.2832]},{"kind":"point","id":"crest_a","at":{"on":"wave_a","x":1.5708}},{"kind":"point","id":"crest_sum","at":{"on":"double_wave","x":1.5708}},{"kind":"measure","from":[1.5708,0],"to":{"at":"crest_a"},"label":"A"},{"kind":"measure","from":[2,0],"to":[2,2],"label":"2A"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"constructive","anchor":{"el":"matching","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"y_1=y_2=A\\sin(kx),\\qquad y_1+y_2=2A\\sin(kx)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"4170c93e-b82a-4dad-a326-f2ed38e1a03e","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Now reverse the second wave. Its trough meets the first wave's crest: their displacements are equal in size but opposite in sign.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"invert","on":"opposing","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"negative_wave","y":"-sin(x)","domain":[0,6.2832],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"point","id":"positive_crest","at":{"on":"wave_b","x":1.5708},"label":"+A"},{"kind":"point","id":"negative_trough","at":{"on":"negative_wave","x":1.5708},"label":"−A"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"positive_crest"},"to":{"at":"negative_trough"},"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What is the total displacement where these two marked points overlap?","expected_gist":"Zero, because +A and −A cancel."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"4170c93e-b82a-4dad-a326-f2ed38e1a03e","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Equal-amplitude waves exactly out of phase cancel everywhere. This is complete destructive interference; unequal amplitudes would leave a nonzero resultant.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"cancel","on":"opposing","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"zero_wave","y":"0","domain":[0,6.2832],"label":"resultant"},{"kind":"point","at":[1.5708,0],"label":"0"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"destructive","anchor":{"el":"opposing","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"y_1=A\\sin(kx),\\quad y_2=-A\\sin(kx)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y_1+y_2=0"}],"checkpoint":null}}
